I apologize for the confusion earlier. You're correct that the `findCEO()` function as written earlier returns a JSON object, which can be more difficult to work with in Google Sheets. Here's an updated version of the function that returns the CEO's full name and LinkedIn profile URL as separate values, without using JSON:
```javascript
/**
 * Find CEO's full name and LinkedIn profile URL from company domain
 *
 * @param {string} domain the domain name of the company
 * @return an array with the CEO's full name and LinkedIn profile URL
 * @customfunction
 */
function findCEO(domain) {
  var apiKey = "YOUR API KEY";
  var cx = "YOUR CX";
  var query = "site:linkedin.com/in intitle:ceo " + domain;

  var options = {
    "method": "get",
    "contentType": "application/json"
  };

  var response = UrlFetchApp.fetch("https://www.googleapis.com/customsearch/v1?key=" + apiKey + "&cx=" + cx + "&q=" + encodeURIComponent(query), options);
  var items = JSON.parse(response.getContentText()).items;

  var fullName = "";
  var linkedInUrl = "";

  if (items && items.length > 0) {
    var title = items[0].title.toLowerCase();
    if (title.indexOf("ceo") !== -1 && title.indexOf(domain) !== -1) {
      var regex = / - (.+) \| LinkedIn/;
      var matches = regex.exec(title);
      if (matches && matches.length > 1) {
        fullName = matches[1];
        linkedInUrl = items[0].link;
      }
    }
  }

  return [[fullName, linkedInUrl]];
}
```
This updated version of the function returns the CEO's full name and LinkedIn profile URL as separate values, in a format that can be easily displayed in Google Sheets. The results are returned as a two-column array, where the first column contains the full name, and the second column contains the LinkedIn profile URL.

Make sure to replace the "YOUR API KEY" and "YOUR CX" placeholders with your own API key and custom search engine ID, respectively. Also, make sure that your domain name is in the correct format (e.g. "example.com").

